Might be a little, I think.  
One compromise is to lazy calculate them (because `EqualConditions` won't be changed after LogicalOptimization), like:
```golang
func (p *LogicalJoin) GetJoinKeys() (leftKeys, rightKeys []*expression.Column) {
  if len(p.LeftJoinKeys) > 0 {
    return p.LeftJoinKeys, p.RightJoinKeys
  }
  for _, expr := range p.EqualConditions {
    leftKeys = append(leftKeys, expr.GetArgs()[0].(*expression.Column))
    rightKeys = append(rightKeys, expr.GetArgs()[1].(*expression.Column))
  }
  p.LeftJoinKeys = leftKeys
  p.RightJoinKeys = rightKeys
  return
}
```

But it really increases the code complexity.
